@topic start
@title Getting Started
@level 1
@related controls,survival,gamemodes,about
# Welcome to Cliderness
Cliderness is a free-form open world survival sandbox by korscripterdev. There is no story you must follow and no ending: explore, gather, mine, build and survive however you like.

# Your first minutes
1. Choose New World on the title screen, keep or change the name and seed, pick a game mode and press Enter.
2. You are the white @ in the middle of the map. Walk with W A S D.
3. Watch the panel on the right: health, stamina, hunger, thirst, fatigue and body temperature.
4. Face a tree or rock and press F to mine it. Resources go to your hotbar at the bottom.
5. Select a block with 1-9 and press R to place it.
6. Press Esc to pause and save. Press F1 at any time for this help.

The world is generated from the seed, so the same seed always gives the same world. Only your changes are saved.

@topic controls
@title Controls
@level 1
@related movement,keybinds,inventory,commands
# Default keys
> W A S D / arrows   move
> Shift + W A S D    sprint
> Ctrl (tap) or X    crouch on / off
> Right Shift or V   look at the sky
> F                  attack the creature / mine the block you face
> R                  place the selected block
> >                  dig down (descend)
> <                  build up under yourself (ascend)
> E                  interact: open, trade, board / leave a ship, pick up items / respawn
> U                  use: eat, drink water
> Q / Ctrl+Q         drop one item / the whole stack
> Tab or I           inventory
> C                  character sheet
> K                  crafting
> 1-9                select hotbar slot
> P                  switch status panel page
> F1 or ?            help (pauses the game)
> Esc                pause menu (pauses the game)
> / or T             command console

Every key can be changed in Settings > Key bindings. Terminals on Linux, macOS and Unix cannot report Right Shift or Ctrl pressed alone; use the alternative keys V and X there. Windows and DOS support both.

@topic movement
@title Movement
@level 1
@related sprint,crouch,stamina,terrain
# Walking
Each key press or key repeat moves you one cell. You face the direction you last moved; the cell in front of you is highlighted.

# Height
The world is 3D but shown from above. A cell drawn as a bright floor one level higher is a step: walk into it to climb up (costs stamina). Shaded cells (light shade = 2 high, dark shade = 3 or more) are cliffs you cannot climb directly; dig steps into them or build up with <.

Walking off an edge makes you fall. Falls of 4 or more blocks hurt unless you land in water.

# Swimming
Over water you float on the surface and swim. Swimming is slower and uses a little stamina, and trains the Swimming skill.

@topic sprint
@title Sprinting
@level 2
@related stamina,movement,stats
Hold Shift while moving (or type capital W A S D) to sprint. Sprinting is about twice as fast as walking but costs stamina every step and makes a lot of noise. When stamina is too low you fall back to walking.

Sprinting during a game minute doubles hunger loss for that minute.

@topic crouch
@title Crouching
@level 2
@related movement,stealth
Tap Ctrl (Windows, DOS) or press X to toggle crouching. You move at about half speed but make very little noise (10% instead of 30%). Stamina regenerates faster while crouching or standing still.

Crouching lets you sneak past sleeping or distant creatures.

@topic stealth
@title Noise and Stealth
@level 3
@related crouch,monsters,ai
The status panel shows your current noise: walking 30%, sprinting 80%, swimming 40%, crouching 10%, standing still slowly drops to 0%; attacking makes 60%. Creatures hear you within hearing radius x noise / 100 cells. Crouching also lowers how far they can see you by a third. Darkness, fog and storms shorten sight.

@topic stamina
@title Stamina
@level 2
@related sprint,survival,stats
Stamina (0-100%) is used by sprinting, climbing steps, swimming and mining. It regenerates every tick you are not sprinting or swimming:
> regen per tick = 3 + END / 5  (+2 when idle or crouching)
Regeneration is halved when you are starving or dehydrated. When fatigue rises above 80% your maximum stamina drops to 60%. Some weather (wind, storms, blizzards) drains extra stamina every minute.

@topic survival
@title Survival Basics
@level 1
@related stamina,items,time,weather
# Meters
> HP       health; at 0 you die
> Hunger   100% = full; eat food with U
> Thirst   100% = hydrated; face water and press U to drink
> Fatigue  rises slowly while awake; sleep in a bed to recover
> Temp     body temperature; keep it near 37 C

Hunger and thirst fall over game time. At 0 you lose health. You slowly regenerate health while both are above 60%.

# Death
When you die your items drop where you fell. Press E or Enter to respawn at your spawn point and go back for them.

# Temperature
Cold biomes, winter, night, altitude, wind and water cool you; deserts, summer and heat waves warm you. Below 35.0 C or above 39.5 C you take damage every minute. Willpower slows the change.

@topic gathering
@title Gathering
@level 1
@related mining,items,blocks
Face a plant, tree or block and press F. Plants break instantly, trees and rock take longer. Drops go straight into your inventory; if it is full they fall to the ground.
> Tall grass, ferns, reeds  -> plant fiber
> Bushes, leaves, dead bushes -> sticks
> Berry bushes -> berries (food)
> Trees -> logs, cactus -> cactus fruit
> Jungle leaves -> coconuts
Food can be eaten with U.

@topic mining
@title Mining
@level 2
@related gathering,durability,skills,caves
Press F to mine the block you face. If that cell is empty, you dig into the floor in front of you. Press > to dig the floor under you and descend.

Mining takes time (see the bar at the bottom of the panel). Moving cancels it. Bedrock cannot be broken.

# Tools
Pickaxes speed up rock and ore, axes wood, shovels soil and sand. Without the right tool, blocks that need a pickaxe or axe take three times longer. Tools lose durability with each block.

Ores are found underground: coal, iron, copper and tin in stone; silver, gold, crystals and gems in deep rock below height 24.

@topic building
@title Building Tools
@level 3
@related commands,cheats
Besides placing blocks by hand, cheat commands build whole shapes around you. Every one can be undone with /undo.
> /sphere <block> <r> [hollow]     /dome <block> <r>
> /cylinder <block> <r> <h> [hollow]   /pyramid <block> <r> [hollow]
> /walls <block> <r> <h>   /circle <block> <r>   /platform <block> <r>
> /box <block> <x> [y] [z]   /pillar <block> <h>
> /line, /wall, /bridge, /stairs <block> <length>, /tunnel <length> [w] [h] in the facing direction
> /carve [r]   hollow out a cave
> /fill, /replace, /clone, /setblock, /flatten, /raise, /lower, /smooth, /drain, /flood

@topic crafting
@title Crafting, Smelting and Cooking
@level 2
@related stations,farming,equipment,durability
Press K to open crafting. The list shows every recipe; bright entries can be made now. Tab shows only craftable recipes, C cycles categories, / searches.
> Enter  craft once
> A      craft as many as possible
> R      repair the item held in the hotbar

A recipe can need: ingredients, a station nearby (within 3 cells), fuel units and a minimum skill level. Hand recipes need no station: planks from logs, sticks, a workbench, campfires, torches, cloth and rope.

# Smelting and cooking
The furnace turns raw ores into ingots, sand into glass, clay into bricks and logs into charcoal. The campfire cooks bread, baked potatoes, stews and herbal tea. Both burn fuel: coal and charcoal give 8 units, logs 4, planks 2, sticks, cloth and wheat 1.

Crafting trains the Crafting skill, cooking the Cooking skill. Creative mode crafts for free. Recipes are data files in data/recipes.

@topic combat
@title Combat
@level 2
@related monsters,equipment,skills,formulas
Face a creature and press F to attack it (F mines blocks when no creature is in front of you). The status panel shows the name and health of the creature you face.
> Fists      2 damage
> Tools      3 damage
> Swords     4-8 damage (wood, stone, bronze, iron), spear 5
> Bow        hold it and press F: shoots an arrow up to 12 cells in the facing direction (6 damage); arrows are used up, half of the hits can be picked up again
Every weapon has an attack cooldown; swinging costs stamina in survival. Luck gives a chance of a critical hit (double damage). Killing creatures trains Melee or Ranged.

Creatures hit you when they stand next to you. Worn armour reduces the damage and wears down. Some creatures cause bleeding or poison. /threat tells whether a hostile creature is near; you cannot sleep with one within 12 cells.

@topic monsters
@title Creatures
@level 2
@related combat,stealth,trading,difficulty
Creatures appear around you, 16-30 cells away, depending on the biome, the time of day and whether you are underground or at sea. They disappear when you go far away.
> Animals   deer, rabbits, sheep and chickens flee; boars and bears fight back; wolves hunt at night; fish and sharks live in water; bats in caves
> Monsters  zombies, skeletons, bog wraiths and frost ghouls come out at night and burn in daylight; spiders, cave crawlers and slimes live in caves; sand scorpions in deserts
> NPCs      wandering traders and hermits on the surface, old miners in caves (see trading)
Creatures drop meat, leather, bones, feathers, wool, string and more. Cook raw meat and fish at a campfire: raw meat can cause food poisoning.
/creatures lists all types, /bestiary <type> shows details, /mobs lists the living ones.

@topic items
@title Items
@level 2
@related inventory,blocks,durability
Every block that can be picked up is also an item. Other items are resources (coal, raw metals, crystals, gems, fossils), materials (sticks, plant fiber), food and tools.

Items have weight and volume. Your carry capacity is 20 kg + 3 kg per Strength point; carrying more slows you down.

Dropped items lie in the world as entities. In water they float when lighter than water (wood, plant fiber, food) and sink to the bottom when heavier (stone, metal). Dropped items disappear after 20 real minutes.

@topic blocks
@title Blocks and Symbols
@level 2
@related items,terrain,biomes
All graphics are CP437 characters in 16 VGA colours.
> @   you
> ♠   tree (oak, birch, jungle, mangrove)
> ▲   pine tree
> ♣   leaves, bush
> ¥   tall grass
> *   flower
> ~   water (blue), lava (red)
> ≈   mud
> ░   sand, snow, ice
> .   grass or dirt floor
> ·   stone floor
> ▒ ▓ cliffs 2 or 3+ blocks above you
> ♦   ore (colour = mineral)
> ☼   coral
Block appearance is defined in data/blocks.

@topic inventory
@title Inventory and Hotbar
@level 1
@related items,controls
You have 36 slots; slots 1-9 are the hotbar at the bottom of the screen. Open the inventory with Tab.
> Enter      pick up a stack, Enter again to move / swap / merge
> 1-9        move the selected stack to that hotbar slot
> Q / Ctrl+Q drop one / the whole stack
> S          sort the backpack (merges stacks)
> X          split a stack in half
> /          search by name
> F          filter by item type
> U          use (hotbar slots)
The right side shows weight, volume, whether the item floats, durability and food value.

@topic stats
@title Character Attributes
@level 3
@related skills,formulas
Open the character sheet with C. Attributes start at 10.
> STR  carry capacity, mining speed
> DEX  movement speed
> INT  experience gain
> PER  sight radius at night and underground
> CON  maximum HP = 80 + 2 x CON
> END  stamina regeneration and sprint cost
> WIL  resistance to cold and heat
> LCK  chance of an extra drop when mining
Attributes grow through play up to 30 (the bar next to each value shows the training):
> STR  mining, woodcutting, melee      DEX  archery, stealth
> INT  crafting, cooking              PER  exploring, fishing
> END  building, swimming, sailing    CON  surviving damage
> WIL  enduring cold, heat, hunger and thirst
> LCK  opening loot crates, critical hits
The next point needs 60 x the current value of training. /attribute sets a value directly (cheat, up to 100).

@topic skills
@title Skills
@level 3
@related stats,mining,formulas,fishing
Skills grow by doing:
> Mining, Woodcutting   breaking natural blocks
> Building              placing blocks
> Crafting, Cooking     recipes at stations and campfires
> Melee, Ranged         fighting creatures
> Swimming, Sailing     moving through water, travelling by ship
> Fishing               casting a fishing rod
> Stealth               sneaking (crouched) near creatures that are awake
> Exploration           reaching new distances from spawn
Every skill also trains an attribute (see Character Attributes).

Level = square root of (XP / 10), up to 100. Mining and woodcutting levels make breaking blocks faster, Melee and Ranged raise damage, Sailing speeds up ships, Fishing raises the catch chance and shortens the wait, Stealth makes crouching quieter and harder to see.

Anti-farming rules: blocks placed by a player give no XP, exploration XP is only given for reaching new distances from spawn, and at most 60 XP can be earned per game minute.

@topic time
@title Time, Days and Seasons
@level 2
@related weather,sky,pause
Game time is separate from real time. By default one game day lasts 20 real minutes (1 real second = 72 game seconds). Change it in Settings, with /daylength, or speed it up with /timescale.

A year has 12 months of 30 days and four seasons of 90 days, starting in spring. Days are longer in summer. The moon has 8 phases over 30 days.

Night is dark: you only see a few cells around you, more under a full moon. Time stops completely while help (F1) or the pause menu (Esc) is open.

@topic pause
@title Pausing
@level 1
@related time,saving
Opening help (F1, ?) or the pause menu (Esc) freezes the game: time, weather, survival meters, physics and everything else stop. The menus themselves still work. When you close them the game continues exactly where it stopped; the real time you spent paused is not added.

The inventory, character sheet, console and sky view do NOT pause the game.

@topic weather
@title Weather
@level 2
@related time,sky,survival,wind
There are 21 weather types: clear, cloudy, drizzle, rain, heavy rain, thunderstorm, fog, strong wind, storm, snow, heavy snow, blizzard, hail, sandstorm, heat wave, cold snap and volcanic ash in the overworld; the Ancient Storm, Starfall, Silent Mist and Aether Wind in the Ancient Dimension.

Which weather can happen depends on the climate where you are: no snow in hot deserts, sandstorms only in dry heat. Weather changes visibility, temperature, movement speed, stamina and the sky. Rain and snow are animated on the map when you are outdoors.

Rain fills dents in the ground with water and makes crops grow faster; poor visibility shortens how far creatures see; creatures wander less in rain; wind drives sails and raises waves that damage small boats.

@topic sky
@title Looking at the Sky
@level 2
@related time,weather,ancient
Press Right Shift (or V) to look up. The sky shows the sun moving from sunrise to sunset, the moon and its phase, seeded stars and constellations (N shows their names), the Milky Way, clouds drifting with the wind, birds with flapping wings, crows (~), meteors, rare comets, aurora in cold regions, solar and lunar eclipses, sunrise and sunset colours and lightning during storms.

The Ancient Dimension has its own sky: a magenta day, magenta and cyan stars, the aether veil rippling across every night, the ringed world low in the south, and Veyra, a second moon that runs through its eight phases in three days. There are no birds there.

The constellations depend on the world seed. Time keeps running while you look up; F1 or Esc pause as usual.

@topic biomes
@title Biomes
@level 3
@related terrain,ocean,caves
Land: plains, grassland, deciduous forest, conifer forest, rainforest, bamboo forest, desert, savanna, swamp, wetland, tundra, snowfield, alpine highlands, volcanic land, flower field, wasteland.
Coast: sand beach, gravel shore, rocky shore, mudflat, mangrove coast.
Ocean: shallow sea, coral reef, open sea, deep ocean, ocean trench, frozen sea.
Caves: stone, limestone, dripstone, crystal, ice, fungal, fossil, lava and deep bedrock caves, underground lakes and rivers.

Biomes are chosen from temperature, humidity and height noise. Use /biome to see where you are and /locate <biome> to find one. Biomes are defined in data/biomes.

@topic terrain
@title Terrain and the 3D World
@level 3
@related movement,building,caves
The world is made of blocks in three dimensions: x (east), z (south) and y (height, 0-127). Sea level is 62. You see the level you stand on: walls at eye level, the floor below, and lower ground in darker colours.

Everything can be changed: dig holes and tunnels, cut mountains, fill the sea, build islands. Use > and < to go down and up, or dig stairs.

Bedrock at height 0 cannot be broken.

@topic caves
@title Caves and Ores
@level 3
@related mining,terrain,biomes
Caves are carved by 3D noise: large chambers and long winding tunnels. Deep below height 10 they fill with lava. Underground you only see a few cells around you (more with Perception); lava, torches and crystals give light.

Coal, iron, copper and tin appear in stone; silver, gold, crystal clusters and gems in deep rock (below 24). Fossils hide in stone between 24 and 60.

@topic ocean
@title Oceans, Coasts and Rivers
@level 3
@related biomes,fluids,ships
Oceans cover low areas below sea level 62: shallow seas with seaweed, colourful coral reefs in warm water, open sea, deep ocean and trenches. Cold seas freeze at the surface.

Rivers wind through low land and are cut slightly below sea level, so they are filled with water and connect lakes and the sea. Deep caves sometimes hold underground lakes.

You can swim on the surface. Rafts and boats are described under Ships.

@topic fluids
@title Flowing Water and Lava
@level 3
@related ocean,terrain,buckets,weather
ClideFluid moves water and lava. Every fluid cell has a level from 1 to 15 (15 = full block); shallow cells are drawn with a wave sign.
> Fluid falls straight down first, filling the cell below.
> It runs over edges and into holes, even as a thin film.
> On flat ground neighbouring cells share their water until they differ by at most one level, so pools settle and become calm.
> The amount of water is conserved: digging a channel moves water, it does not create or destroy it.
Exceptions: the sea and lakes below sea level refill themselves, so an ocean cannot be drained by a ditch; rain adds water to dents in the ground; water touching lava turns it into obsidian (full lava) or cobblestone.

Build dams with any solid block; break them to flood the valley behind. Lava is slower and thicker than water.

Only cells next to a change are simulated; still water costs nothing. /fluid shows statistics, /settle calms everything, /volume measures.

@topic ships
@title Ships
@level 2
@related sailing,shipdamage,ocean,crafting
Four ships can be crafted (category Vehicles):
> Raft          6 logs + 2 rope; 1 cell, paddled, 220 kg, no hold
> Rowing Boat   workbench; 2 cells, rowed, 400 kg, cargo hold
> Sailing Boat  workbench, Crafting 8; 3 cells, sails + oars, 900 kg
> Merchant Ship workbench, Crafting 15; 5 x 3 cells, sails only, 4000 kg
Hold the ship, face open water and press R to launch it (the stern starts next to you). Press E next to it to board, E again to leave: you step ashore when there is land next to the hull, otherwise you jump into the water. F on an empty ship while you are ashore hauls it back into your inventory; the item keeps the hull damage.

Aboard, R opens the cargo hold (27 slots, limited by the ship's capacity) and U bails water or, while holding the repair material (logs for rafts, planks for boats), patches the hull.

@topic ancient
@title The Ancient Dimension
@level 2
@related portal,ancientbiomes,monsters,weather
The only other dimension. Its sky glows magenta by day; its weather includes Starfall, Silent Mist, Aether Wind and the Ancient Storm (no rain or snow there). Terrain, caves and seas are generated like the overworld but with their own seed, blocks and eight biomes (see Ancient Biomes).
> Ores      Aether veins (8-90) and Starmetal (4-40) in Ancient Stone
> Plants    glowcaps, star flowers, crystal blooms, moonreed, elder trees (glowing leaves)
> Creatures star stags, glow moths; void wisps and silent stalkers at night; ancient guardians near sanctuaries and rifts; crystal golems in caves; sanctuary keepers trade relics
> Structures Ancient Cities (walled, with streets, houses and coffers) and Ancient Shrines
Ancient recipes need the Ancient Altar (6 ancient bricks + 4 aether shards at a workbench): starmetal tools, sword and armour, Aether Elixir (Ancient Blessing), Aether Lanterns. Ancient Relics grant the Ancient Blessing when used.
/dimension, /dimensions and /ancientbiomes show where you are.

@topic durability
@title Durability and Repair
@level 3
@related mining,items,crafting
Tools have durability. Each block mined with the right tool costs 1 point, with the wrong tool 2; hoes lose 1 per tilled cell. At 10% you get a warning; at 0 the tool breaks. The hotbar shows the remaining durability.

Repair: stand near a workbench or anvil, hold the item and press R in the crafting screen. One unit of the repair material (planks for wooden tools, cobblestone for stone, iron ingots for iron, cloth for clothes) restores 25-50% plus 2% per Crafting level.
> /durability set <percent>   /repair

@topic gamemodes
@title Game Modes
@level 2
@related cheats,survival
> Survival   all survival meters, limited resources
> Creative   no hunger or damage, instant mining, blocks are not used up
> Adventure  survival rules but you cannot break or place blocks
> Spectator  fly through everything, no world changes
Change with /gamemode (needs cheats). Cheats are a separate world setting.

@topic cheats
@title Cheats
@level 3
@related commands,gamemodes
Cheat commands (marked [cheat] in the command list) need cheats to be enabled for the world: when creating it, or with /cheats on. Using any cheat marks the world as cheated; this is saved.

God mode (/god), flying (/fly) and walking through blocks (/noclip) are flags independent of the game mode.

@topic commands
@title All Commands
@level 3
@related cheats,controls,building,waypoints
Open the console with / (or T). Type a command and press Enter. Tab completes command names and arguments (blocks, items, weather, modes...), Up/Down recall earlier commands, PgUp/PgDn scroll the output. Coordinates accept ~ for "relative to me", e.g. /tp ~10 ~ ~.

There are 240 commands in groups: player, position, movement, actions (mine, place, use, interact, step, face: everything the keys do), items and storage, crafting, equipment, building and shapes, world and terrain, time, weather, fluids, creatures and combat, ships, dimensions, interface and help, system. /commands lists them, /help <command> explains one, /aliases lists short names, /topics and /helpsearch search the help.

@topic saving
@title Saving and Loading
@level 2
@related pause,troubleshooting
Save with Esc > Save or /save; leaving to the menu also saves. Worlds are stored in saves/<name>/:
> world.ini   seed, time, weather, player, inventory, items
> chunks.dat  only the chunks you changed
Every save is written to a temporary file, read back and checked (CRC-32) before it replaces the old save, so a crash while saving cannot destroy your previous save. Load worlds from the title screen.

@topic settings
@title Settings
@level 2
@related languages,keybinds
Settings are stored in clide.ini next to the game:
> Language       see the language list
> Key bindings   change any key (two keys per action)
> Sound          on / off
> Day length     10 to 60 real minutes per game day

@topic keybinds
@title Changing Keys
@level 2
@related settings,controls
Settings > Key bindings lists every action. Enter sets the first key, A the second, Del clears the second, R restores the defaults. Bindings are saved in keys.ini. Shift on a movement key always means sprint.

@topic languages
@title Languages
@level 2
@related settings,troubleshooting
The game detects your system language and uses it if your display can show it. On a CP437 display (DOS) only languages whose text fits CP437 are offered; others are hidden. On Unicode terminals, languages with special scripts (Korean, Japanese, Chinese) are marked unverified because the game cannot know whether your font has the glyphs: choosing one shows a sample and asks you to confirm.

@topic audio
@title Sound
@level 3
@related settings,platforms,weather
ClideAudio generates every sound procedurally (noise generators, oscillators, filters, envelopes) instead of playing recordings, and mixes sounds that overlap.
> Windows        live PCM through waveOut (22050 Hz)
> Linux / BSD    live PCM streamed to PulseAudio (pacat), PipeWire (pw-cat) or ALSA (aplay) when one of them is installed
> macOS          live PCM through afplay (whole sounds, no mixing)
> DOS            PC Speaker tone sequences
You hear footsteps and splashes, mining, breaking, placing, thunder, hull creaks and cracks, and ambience: rain, wind, storms, caves, the drone of the Ancient Dimension, birds by day and crows at dusk.
Without an audio device the game runs silently. Check the device with
> cliderness --play-sfx rain
/audio shows the back end, /sound on|off switches sound, /sounds and /playsound try effects, --render-sfx writes a WAV file.

@topic platforms
@title Platforms
@level 4
@related audio,troubleshooting
The engine is ISO C90. Supported: Windows console, Linux, macOS and other Unix terminals (UTF-8), MS-DOS and FreeDOS (VGA text mode, 16-bit Open Watcom or DJGPP builds). All platforms use the same 80x25 CP437 grid and 16 colours; the exact glyph shapes depend on the terminal font.

On DOS the world cache is smaller (40 chunks) and memory use is capped to fit in 640 KB.

@topic troubleshooting
@title Troubleshooting
@level 3
@related platforms,languages,saving
> Boxes or ? instead of symbols: use a font with box drawing characters (Consolas, DejaVu Sans Mono), or Windows Terminal.
> Screen too small: the game needs at least 80x25 characters; a red notice shows the current size.
> Korean/Japanese/Chinese text broken: your font lacks the glyphs; choose another language or font.
> Right Shift / Ctrl do nothing: your terminal cannot report them; use V and X.
> "data error" at start: run the game from its folder or pass --data <dir>.
> Load fails with CRC mismatch: world.ini was edited or damaged.
> No sound: normal without a supported device; check Settings > Sound.

@topic world
@title World Generation
@level 4
@related terrain,biomes,formulas
The world is generated from the seed with integer value noise, so it is identical on every platform, including 16-bit DOS without a floating point unit. Height comes from a continental layer (256-block features), detail (32 blocks) and mountains. Temperature, humidity and a special layer pick the biome. 3D noise carves caves; ores are placed by per-block hash.

The world is stored in 32x32 chunks, one height level each. Only chunks you change are saved.

@topic advanced
@title Advanced Mechanics
@level 4
@related formulas,world,time
The simulation runs at a fixed 20 ticks per second. Real time is accumulated and converted into ticks only while no pausing screen is open; long stalls are capped at 250 ms so the game never jumps ahead. Game time advances by 50 ms x 86400 / day_length x time_scale per tick (3.6 game seconds by default) with exact remainders, so 20 real minutes are exactly one game day.

Movement waits for a per-step cooldown in ticks: walk 4, sprint 2, crouch 7, swim 6, multiplied by 100 / (90 + DEX). Slowing blocks add 3, overloading adds 2 or 6, weather adds its move penalty in percent.

@topic formulas
@title Formulas (Master)
@level 5
@related advanced,stats,skills
> mining ticks = hardness (x3 without a needed tool, or x100/power with the right tool), then x100/(100+2 STR), then x100/(100+3 skill)
> skill level = isqrt(xp / 10); xp per natural block = hardness/4 + 1, x(100 + 3 INT)/130, max 60 per minute
> sprint cost = 12 x 100 / (80 + 2 END) per step
> stamina regen = 3 + END/5 per tick (+2 idle)
> hunger: -0.5 per game minute (-1.0 if you sprinted), thirst -0.7 (-1.0 above 28 C), fatigue +0.4
> body temperature drift = |env - limit| x 10 / (20 + WIL) tenths of a degree per minute outside 10..28 C
> fall damage = (blocks - 3) x 8 for falls of 4+
> carry capacity = 20 kg + 3 kg x STR
> sight radius = 4 + visibility x 32 / 100 + PER/3 outdoors, 4 + PER/4 underground
> melee damage = weapon x (100 + 3 STR) / 130 x (100 + 2 Melee) / 100, x2 on a critical hit (chance = LCK %), minus the creature's defense (min 1)
> bow damage = 6 x (100 + 2 Ranged) / 100, range 12
> creature damage = damage x difficulty (50/100/150%) x (100 - armour defense) / 100, min 1
> creature spawning: every 2 s, 16-30 cells away, cap 16 + 6 x difficulty (DOS 8 + difficulty)

@topic about
@title About Cliderness
@level 1
@related start,platforms
